L1: This study was aimed to examine the effect of vitamin C/E intake alone or combined with resistance training on antioxidant/pro-oxidant status, muscle strength and body composition in an elderly population. Fifty-seven men and women with a mean age of 65.6 ± 3.8 years were recruited and randomized in a double-blind fashion into four groups: control-placebo; resistance training (RT); vitamins C/E supplementation (AS); AS+RT. Oxidative stress status and metabolic and lipid profiles were determined at baseline and after six months. Fat-free mass and fat mass measured by DXA were similar at baseline for all groups. At six month, there was a significant difference among the groups as a function of vitamin E supplementation. Moreover, although there was no effect on pro-oxidative parameters, a significant effect on body composition was noted, but no difference was noted on strength gain. The combination of RT+AS had a positive effect on the plasma antioxidant profile but not on the pro-oxidant status.
L2: Aging is associated with biochemical modifications and an increase in free radical production as caused by an alteration of the mitochondrial respiratory chain (1). These age-related changes also lead to a decrease in the functional capacity of the elderly (2) while the imbalance between pro-oxidants and antioxidants leads to an increase in oxidative stress (3) which is believed to be a major contributor to several age-related diseases (4). Since these diseases are a major cause of disability and death (5) in the elderly and may develop into a pandemic in the coming years (6), it is important to develop effective prevention strategies.
L3: Regular physical exercise is critically important for the health and well-being of people of all ages. While an acute exercise session seems to induce oxidative stress (alteration of the balance between pro-oxidant and antioxidant systems) (7) regular exercise training tends to have a beneficial action on this balance (8). However, while some studies have shown that resistance training (RT) may induce a decrease in oxidative stress in younger adults (9), these effects seem to be altered by aging (10). Biochemical changes may explain these discrepancies (11). More specifically, the functionality of the enzymatic system decreases with aging (12). As such, RT coupled with antioxidant supplementation (AS) might be more beneficial than RT alone, especially in elderly populations (13).
L4: Indeed, AS may be an alternative to improving the balance between pro-oxidants and antioxidants and up-regulating enzymatic systems. It has been shown that vitamin C and/or E supplementation, two major antioxidants, can have a positive influence on antioxidant/pro-oxidant status (14). Elderly people are at higher risk of developing oxidative stress conditions due to lower plasma levels of antioxidants, especially vitamins C and E (12). AS has been shown to increase plasma levels of these antioxidants (15) and improve the action of antioxidant enzymes 16, 17. It can also reduce atherosclerosis development by decreasing the susceptibility of lipoproteins to lipid peroxidation 18, 19 and increase the beneficial effects of physical exercise by upregulating angiogenesis and nitric oxide bioavailability (20). These facts support the hypothesis that RT has a more pronounced effect when combined with AS.
L5: Although some studies have investigated the effect of vitamins and exercise in an elderly population, they were limited to acute exercise combined to short supplementation with vitamins. Sacheck et al. used a high dose of vitamin E (1000 IU/d) combined to eccentric exercise and shown that vitamin E supplementation induced modest changes eccentric exercise-induced oxidative stress (21). A study of Latham et al. investigated the effect of vitamin D supplementation on the risk of falling and concluded that vitamin D alone cannot be recommended for use in clinical practice where the primary aim is to improve muscle strength or physical function or reduce the risk of falling in frail older people (22). Evans highlighted the beneficial effect of vitamin E or C towards the reduction of oxidative damage and the skeletal muscle repair and suggested to investigate the effect of combining vitamin E and C on these parameters (23). The group of Fiatarone et al. investigated the effects of macronutrients in frail elderly subjects and concluded that high-intensity resistance exercise training is a feasible and effective means of counteracting muscle weakness and physical frailty in very elderly people (24). In addition to these previous studies, the raised question is whether AS combined with RT in the elderly has a positive effect on the antioxidant/pro-oxidant balance which, when perturbed, can predispose individuals to oxidative damage and oxidative stress-related pathologies, especially the elderly. The aim of our randomized, double-blind, controlled trial was thus to determine whether six months of RT combined with AS would improve the antioxidant/pro-oxidant balance and body composition in a healthy older population.
L6: Fifty-seven subjects (27 men and 30 women) aged between 59 and 73 (65.6 ± 3.8 yrs) were recruited for the study using advertisements in local newspapers. The inclusion criteria were as follows: healthy, no major physical incapacity, sedentary, stable weight (± 2 kg) for the last six months, non-smoker, moderate drinker (maximum 15 g of alcohol/day or the equivalent of one alcoholic beverage/day), no medication that could have an impact on lipid and glucose metabolisms, no menstruations for the preceding 12 months, and no hormonal replacement therapy for women. The sample size was calculated to ensure statistical power when comparing groups (= 0.05; = 0.80), based on an expected difference of 1 kg of muscle mass at the end of the study. This average gain was determined from results obtained in previous studies that have demonstrated such effect 25, 26. In addition, the retention rate observed was 85% while it was expected to be 80%. This rate was based on the study of Lemmer et al. who assessed exercises conducted in older women (26). This sample size would therefore allow to complete the study with a minimum of 16 participants per group.
L7: A phone interview was conducted to screen for the inclusion criteria. After explaining the nature and goals of the study to the subjects, they were asked to come to the Research Center on Aging (Sherbrooke University Geriatric Institute). Upon arrival, they provided written informed consent. Their resting energy expenditure (REE) was then measured and a 12-hour fasting blood sample was collected. After eating breakfast, their body weight, height, and body composition (DXA) were measured and instructions were provided for keeping their dietary record. The subjects were then asked to complete a three-day dietary record to assess their dietary intakes. At the end of the visit, the subjects were randomized into four groups: (1) placebo, (2) resistance training (RT), (3) vitamin C and E supplementation (AS), and (4) vitamin C and E supplementation combined with resistance training (AS + RT). The double-blind randomization was done by Arkopharma Ltd. The pharmacy of the Sherbrooke University Geriatric Institute provided the vitamin C and E supplements. The subjects were enlisted in this study all over the year. The procedures and protocol were approved by the Ethics Committee of the Sherbrooke University Geriatric Institute. All the following described measurements were performed before and after the intervention period.
L8: The subjects ingested either vitamin C (ascorbate) (1,000 mg; 2 capsules of 500 mg) and E (dl- -tocopherol) (400 IU; 2 capsules of 300 mg) capsules or placebos (lactose: 100 mg) of similar size and appearance daily for six months. The vitamin C capsules were obtained from the Sherbrooke University Geriatric Institute pharmacy (Wampole Brands Inc., Toronto, ON, Canada) while the vitamin E and placebos (for both vitamins) capsules were supplied by Arkopharma Ltd. (Arkopharma Ltd., Carros, France). All capsules (2 capsules) were taken in the morning during breakfast, to avoid loss or omission. All subjects reached compliance minimum participation (95% of the capsules; number of returned capsules and measures of plasma vitamin C and E concentrations) to have their results retained for the study.
L9: The subjects performed RT three days a week (Monday, Wednesday, and Friday) for six months. Each one-hour session involved warm-up, exercise and recovery phases, and included seven exercises for the upper and lower body (leg press, bench press, leg extension, shoulder press, seated row, triceps extension and biceps curl) with three sets of eight repetitions at 80% of 1-RM (monthly measured and adapted) and abdominal exercise (sit up: 3 x 20 repetitions) was also integrated (27). All subjects reached compliance minimum participation (85% of the sessions) to have their results retained for the study. All sessions were supervised by a kinesiologist.
L10: A self administered questionnaire was used to estimate the dietary intakes (28). The subjects were instructed to maintain normal dietary habits throughout the study. They were provided with a five-kg food scale and shown how to complete a three-day dietary record. The results were analyzed using CANDAT SYSTEM version 6.0 software (Candat, London, ON, Canada) to determine daily energy, protein, carbohydrate, and lipid intakes. Protein intakes were subdivided based on the source (animal or vegetable) and lipids were categorized as mono-, poly-, or unsaturated fatty acids.
L11: Determination of fat mass (FM) and fat-free mass (FFM) was assessed in a supine position, by the use of dual energy Xray absorptiometry (DXA; GE Prodigy Lunar, Madison, WI, USA). In our laboratory, the coefficients of variations for repeated measures of FM and FFM in ten adults (measured one week apart) are 0.4 and 0.5%, respectively. FFM is defined herein as the mass of tissue representing soft tissue exclusively (mineral body mass excluded).
L12: Muscle mass is estimated by urinary excretion of creatinine. Measurement of creatinine excretion is one of the least expensive methods, and more valid and safe for the subjects (29) since creatinine is a degradation product of the phospho-creatinine muscle. The formula reported by Welle et al. (29) was used to calculate the muscle mass: Muscle mass (kg) = {[(Urine quantity (mL) / 100) * (Creatinine quantity (mg/dL))] / 1000} * 21.8.
L13: Visceral FM (VFM) was estimated using the equation developed and validated by Bertin et al. (30): 79.6 x {SD – [(TED - TID) / 2] x TID} / H – 149, where SD is the sagittal diameter (cm), TED is the transverse external diameter (cm), TID is the transverse internal diameter (cm) and H is the height (cm). This estimation of VFM by DXA was correlated [r=0.94 (P<0.0001)] with computed tomographic scan data obtained in overweight women.
L14: Blood samples were collected in the morning after a twelve-hour fasting period and were obtained within the week of end of AS or RT. The plasma lipid profile [high-density lipoprotein (HDL), low-density lipoprotein (LDL), total cholesterol, total cholesterol/HDL ratio, triglycerides] was determined in the clinical laboratory of the Centre Hospitalier Universitaire de Sherbrooke as described elsewhere (31). Urine was collected by the participants during three consecutive days. Creatinine was measured by colorimetry as described by Welle et al. (29) (auto-analyzer DCA 2000 +, Bayer, USA).
L15: Vitamin C
L16: Plasma vitamin C (ascorbate) was analyzed in samples that had been conditioned before freezing. A volume of cold, freshly prepared 10% metaphosphoric acid (100 µL) was added to an equal volume of fresh plasma. The mixture was centrifuged (3,500 x g, 15 min, 4°C) and the supernatant was stored at –80°C. Plasma ascorbate levels were analyzed by HPLC using a CSC Hypersil ODS column (5 µm particles, 25 x 0.43 cm i.d.; Thermo Electron Corporation, MA, USA). A Coulochem II 50-10A electrochemical analytical cell (ESA, MA, USA) was used to measure vitamin C concentrations (32).
L17: Plasma vitamin E was analyzed as - and -tocopherol in frozen samples. Briefly, thawed plasma was mixed with an equal volume of ethanol (100 µL). The - and -tocopherol were extracted in hexane (500 µL). Tocopherol acetate was used as an internal standard. Plasma - and -tocopherol were resolved on a Sephasil reverse-phase HPLC column (C18, 5 µm particles, 25 x 0.46 cm i.d.; Pharmacia Biotech, NJ, USA). A Coulochem II 50-10A electrochemical analytical cell (ESA) coupled to a UV photodiode array detector (292 nm, SPD-M10Avp, Shimadzu, MD, USA) was used to measure vitamin E concentrations (18).
L18: TAS levels were determined in frozen plasma using the Trolox equivalent antioxidant assay as described by Miller (33). TAS measures the relative ability of circulating antioxidants to scavenge 2,2’-azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) (ABTS) radical cations (ABTSx+) relative to the antioxidant capacity of Trolox standards. The absorbance was measured at 734 nm (U-3000, Hitachi, ON, CA) and the antioxidant activity of the serum was expressed as mmol/L of Trolox equivalent.
L19: Plasma albumin was measured using the bromocresol purple (BCP) dye-binding method as previously described (34;35). In the presence of a solubilizing agent, BCP binds to albumin at pH 4.9. The amount of albumin-BCP complex (absorbance at 600 nm) is directly proportional to the albumin concentration.
L20: Uric acid: Plasma uric acid was measured as previously described (36). Uric acid, which absorbs light at 293 nm, is converted to allantoin by uricase. The change in absorbance at 293 nm due to the disappearance of uric acid is directly proportional to the concentration of uric acid in the sample and is measured using a bichromatic (293, 700 nm) endpoint technique.
L21: This parameter was determined by subtracting the antioxidant activity attributable to albumin and uric acid from the total antioxidant activity of the plasma samples, which reflects the antioxidant activity of ascorbate, -tocopherol, -carotene, bilirubin, and other radical scavenging antioxidants (33). RAS was determined using the following formula: RAS (mmol/L) = TAS (mmol/L) - [(0.69 x Plasma albumin (mmol/L)) + (Plasma uric acid (mmol/L))] (33). The results are expressed as µM of Trolox equivalent.
L22: Isoprostanes are prostaglandin-like compounds produced by free radical-mediated lipid peroxidation. Urinary 15-isoprostane F2t (also known as 8-epi-PGF2a, 8-iso-PGF2a or 8-isoprostane) was measured using a competitive enzyme-linked immunoassay (ELISA) (Neogen Corporation, KY, USA) with the absorbance of the reader set at 650 nm. Each sample was analyzed in duplicate (Victor™ X5, PerkinElmer, ON, CA). 15-isoprostane F2t concentrations are expressed as nmol 15-isoprostane F2t/mmol creatinine.
L23: The plasma samples were stored at -80°C until analyzed. Thiobarbituric acid-reactive substances, mainly MDA, were assayed using an ODS Hypersil HPLC column (4.6 x 100 mm, 5 µm, Agilent) and an ODS guard column (5 µm) placed in a column warmer set at 37°C. The HPLC set-up was coupled to a fluorescence detector with an excitation wavelength of 515 nm and an emission wavelength of 553 nm (RF-10Axl, Shimadzu, MD, USA; 515 nm) (37). Malondialdehyde concentrations are expressed as µM.
L24: Values are given as mean ± standard deviation (± SD). A one-way ANOVA (with an LSD post-hoc test) was performed to assess differences between 4 groups at baseline. Repeated measure ANOVAs were used to examine the treatment effect on all variables when adjusting for covariates (gender or dietary intake). When the repeated-measure ANOVA was significant, a LSD-multiple comparison post-hoc test was used to locate differences. Independent sample t-tests were used to compare 2 groups at baseline. Paired t-tests were used to assess differences between baseline and post-intervention measurements in each group. The significance was set at P≤0.05. All analyses were performed with the SPSS software version 15.0 (SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA).
