L26: Data of the first table were similar among groups. Moreover, there was no significant gender-, height-, or weight-based differences among the groups (Table 1).
L27: Significant treatment were noted for vitamin E, these 2 forms (- and -tocopherol) and the ratio (-/ - tocopherol), with significant difference among supplemented groups and the non-supplemented groups (P≤0.01). No treatment effect was noted with the vitamin C, TAS, RAS, MDA and F2-isoprostane measurements, even if slight differences were detected by the paired t-test (Table 2).
L28: Significant treatment were observed for appendicular FFM and muscle mass (P≤0.01), with no difference among groups. No treatment effect was noted with the others parameters, even if slight differences were detected by the paired t-test (Table 3). Finally, no differences were observed for nutritional characteristics of interest at baseline and after the intervention period (data not show).
L29: The purpose of the present study was to determine the impact of a combination of AS and RT on the antioxidant/pro-oxidant profile and the body composition and biochemical parameters of a healthy elderly population.
L30: Plasma vitamin C was within the normal range at baseline for all the groups (20.80 ± 14.41 µM) (38) despite a large dose of vitamin C consumption that exceeded the dietary reference intake (152.2 ± 82.6 vs. 75-90 mg). This low plasma concentration of vitamin C may be explained by a poor intestinal absorption of vitamin C (39). After the intervention, for the supplemented groups, there was a significant increase in the concentration of plasma vitamin C, which reached the normal range (from 23.43 ± 14.40 to 49.66 ± 23.83 µM). It is interesting to note that this concentration of plasma vitamin C is similar to that reported in other studies of elderly populations taking the same daily dose of vitamin C (40;41). Nevertheless, while AS doubled the plasma concentration of vitamin C, it is surprising that the concentration was not higher given the large dose of vitamin C. This strengthens the possibility that the absorption of this compound was limited or impaired in our study groups. Nevertheless, it is important to note that intestinal absorption of ascorbic acid is mediated through a sodium-dependent active transport process I and that high doses of vitamin C are probably not as efficiently absorbed as smaller multiple doses of the vitamin (42). Moreover, the cellular transport of vitamin C is regulated under physiological conditions and altered by aging and disease (43). Lastly, the significant increase in plasma vitamin C in the RT group indicates that RT likely had a specific effect.
L31: Vitamins E and C are important plasma and cellular antioxidants. -tocopherol is the major lipophilic chain-breaking antioxidant. Several studies have shown that these two molecules (α-tocopherol and vitamin C) act in synergy to regulate oxidative stress. At baseline, plasma - and -tocopherol concentrations were in the normal range for all the groups (27.78 ± 5.50 and 3.21 ± 1.23 µM, respectively) 44, 45. After the intervention, for the supplemented groups, there was a significant increase in plasma α-tocopherol. Since dietary intakes did not change (data not shown), the increase in plasma α-tocopherol was most likely due to AS. On the other hand, plasma γ-tocopherol decreased significantly for the intervention groups. This has been attributed to a competition effect between α- and γ-tocopherol for the receptor (46) and to an increase in hepatic turnover of γ-tocopherol following a high intake of α-tocopherol (47). In addition, plasma γ-tocopherol as well as the γ-tocopherol / α-tocopherol ratio have been shown to be possible markers of nutrition-related risk (48). Nevertheless, there is significant inter-individual variability in vitamin E (α- and γ-tocopherol) absorption, which may be caused by intrinsic differences in the expression and activity of intestinal vitamin E (49)
L32: TAS value for our subjects as a whole was 1.12 ± 0.10 mmol/L Trolox equivalents, which is low compared to the normal value reported by Miller and Sung et al. (1.31 to 1.58 mmol/L Trolox equivalents) 33, 38. The TAS value remained low, even when corrected as a function of plasma albumin and uric acid, which is defined as RAS. The RAS values were 0.37 ± 0.09 mmol/L Trolox equivalents, which is low compared to those reported by Miller and Sfrent-Cornateanu et al. (0.45 and 0.92 mmol/L Trolox equivalents, respectively) 33, 50. A number of studies have thus shown that TAS decreases during aging and that there is a gender-related difference, with higher values for men than for women, which is in agreement with our results (data not show). After the interventions, there was no significant difference in TAS or RAS.
L33: The mean MDA value was 1.37 ± 0.37 µM, which is in the normal range for healthy subjects (51). While there was a decrease in MDA concentrations in all the groups after the interventions, the decrease was not statistically significant and may be due to the absence of oxidative stress. These results were confirmed by the levels of urinary F2-isoprostanes, especially 8-iso-prostaglandin F2 (52).
L34: While the improvement in the antioxidant/pro-oxidant profile appeared limited, an impact on body composition was observed. Based on the notion that RT induces oxidative stress and damage, which may prevent adaptations to body composition, AS should reduce oxidative stress and damage and enhance RT-related adaptations. While the only change in body composition was observed with the RT groups (appendicular FFM), the synergy between the two interventions improved body composition at multiple levels. In addition to the gain in FFM, which was also reported by Labonté et al., we observed a significant change in appendicular FFM (53). Despite the fact that the RT groups had a similar antioxidant/pro-oxidant profile before and after the intervention, we only observed significant changes in body composition in the combined intervention group. However, AS did not seem to prevent some of the changes in body composition, especially the decline in muscle mass. Besides, significant differences in FM variables were observed in the placebo group (total FM in kilograms and as a percentage; abdominal FM in kilograms and as a percentage), which may have been due to normal weight gain associated with aging over the six-month period. Because an increase in FM, even limited, is a risk factor for developing some pathologies (CVD, type 2 diabetes, obesity) 54, 55, it is particularly important to protect against FM gains using RT and vitamin E and C supplements. Lastly, even if muscle mass showed a treatment effect, we noted no treatment effect on muscle strength, but only a positive significant effect with similar changes between both groups of RT. Like we found no difference on strength gains between the 2 groups after the intervention, AS did not maximize strength gains during RT. Moreover, we found no association between variations of strength and FFM for total, upper, trunk and lower body segments as muscle strength increased to the same extent while appendicular FFM increased significantly (data not show). This result tends to prove that strength gains occurred by other processes (neurologic (56, 57, 58)) rather than by hypertrophy after 6 months. A 6 month RT program, with or without AS, in healthy older individuals has beneficial effects on strength gains. Compare with current literature, strength gain was of the same magnitude observed after RT program (more than 50%) (59).
L35: The lack of a significant treatment effect may be due to the small size of the cohort, which might have prevented statistical significance from being reached. Like subjects were enlisted in this study all over the year, we estimated that seasonal variations that could be occur were omitted. Thus, the numerous analyses conducted with the small sample may have increased the risks of type I error. Given that the subjects had a relatively normal metabolic profile and antioxidant/pro-oxidant status; it was difficult to detect a beneficial effect on biochemical parameters by our interventions. As dietary intakes did not change during the study (data not shown), this factor could be eliminated as a major cause of changes in parameters, so observed changes could be attributed to the interventions related to the study.
L36: In conclusion, while our results demonstrated that AS combined with RT increases the concentrations of plasma antioxidants that are known to have a beneficial effect on the anti- and pro-oxidant profile in the elderly, the effect did not reach statistical significance on this last status. However, the variations in antioxidant levels might have been responsible for the improvements in FFM and FM. High intensity RT and AS were well-tolerated and should be recommended for other purposes, because elderly seem to maintain a high degree of training capacity. Our study was aimed to examine the combination of these interventions in an elderly population. However, further studies with more subjects will be needed to provide statistically significant results and to be able to propose effective interventions involving RT that consistently provide a beneficial effect.
L37: AcknowledgmentsAcknowledgements: We would like to thank all the subjects who have participated in this study as well as Martine Fisch (R.N.) for her valuable assistance. This study was funded by the Canadian Diabetes Association. The vitamin E and placebos were generously supplied by Arkopharma, Ltd. (Carros, France).Conflict of Interest: AK received a salary grant from the FRSQ and IJD from the Canadian Institute of Health Research. FB received a D. Menzies bursary.
